          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo →
                      %mn ∞
                  %mi vol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi N
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 4
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi vol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{N \to \infty}{\lim}
                  vol(S^2_N)
                  \\;=\;
                  4 \pi
                  \\;=\;
                  vol(S^2)
                  \\,.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#ShapeObservablesAsVassilievInvariants Shape observables as weight systems on chord diagrams
          %p
            We discuss how the “shape observables” on the fuzzy 2-sphere (
            %a{:href => "#Fuzzy2SphereDefinition"}> above
            ) are given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/single+trace+observables"} single trace observables
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+weight+systems"} Lie algebra weight systems
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chord+diagrams"} chord diagrams
            (following
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#RamgoolamSpenceThomas04"} Ramgoolam-Spence-Thomas 04
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#McNamaraPapageorgakis05"} McNamara-Papageorgakis 05
            %a{:href => "#McNamara06"} McNamara 06, Section 4
            for review).
          %p
            For more see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+systems+on+chord+diagrams+in+physics"} weight systems on chord diagrams in physics
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/WeightSystemsAsShapeObservabesOnFuzzySphere.jpg", :width => "400"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            While in the commutative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
            function are equal
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo →
                      %mn ∞
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi S
                        %mi N
                        %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 4
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{N\to \infty}{\lim}
                  \\int_{S^2_N} R^{2 k}
                  \\;=\;
                  4 \pi
                  \\,;
          %p
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            there is an ordering ambiguity: In fact, the number of functions on the fuzzy 2-sphere at finite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            that all go to the same function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^{2k}
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            grows rapidly with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            At
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 1
            there is the single radius observable
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RadiusOfFuzzy2Sphere"} (10)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi S
                        %mi N
                        %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi S
                        %mi N
                        %mn 2
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 4
                  %mi π
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi N
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi N
                            %mn 2
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_{S^2_N} R^2
                  \\;=\;
                  \\int_{S^2_N}
                  \\underset{i}{\sum} X_i \cdot X_i
                  \\;=\;
                  4 \pi \tfrac{ N }{ \sqrt{N^2 -1} }
          %p
            At
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 2
            there are, under the integral
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:FuzzyS2Integration"} (11)
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mrow
                          %msubsup
                            %mi S
                            %mi N
                            %mn 2
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{S^2_N} \underset{i,j}{\sum} X_i X_i X_j X_j
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mrow
                          %msubsup
                            %mi S
                            %mi N
                            %mn 2
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ,
                          %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{S^2_N} \underset{i,j}{\sum} X_i X_j X_j X_i
          %p
            (Here we are using that under the integral/trace, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+permutation"} cyclic permutation
            of the factors in the integrand does not change the result).
          %p
            Similarly for higher
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
            that appears here is familiar in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+theory"} knot theory
          %p
            Every ordering of operators, up to cyclic permutation, in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/single+trace+observable"} single trace observable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr(R^2)^n
            is encoded in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chord+diagram"} chord diagram
            and the value of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/single+trace+observable"} single trace observable
            is the value of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/su%282%29"} su(2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+weight+system"} Lie algebra weight system
            on this chord diagram.
          %blockquote
            %p
              from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Differential+Cohomotopy+implies+intersecting+brane+observables"} Sati-Schreiber 19c
          %h2#appearance_in_dbrane_geometry Appearance in D-brane geometry
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+spheres"} fuzzy spheres
            appear in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+geometry"} D-brane geometry
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+funnels"} fuzzy funnels
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dp-D%28p%2B2%29-brane+intersections"} Dp-D(p+2)-brane intersections
                have
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+2-sphere"} fuzzy 2-sphere
                slices
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+funnels"} fuzzy funnels
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dp-D%28p%2B4%29-brane+intersections"} Dp-D(p+4)-brane intersections
                have
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+4-sphere"} fuzzy 4-sphere
                slices
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetric
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
                solutions of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BMN+matrix+model"} BMN matrix model
                are precisely
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+2-sphere"} fuzzy 2-sphere
                configurations (
                %a{:href => "BMN+matrix+model#BerensteinMaldacenaNastase02"}> BMN 02 (5.4)
                ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+funnel"} fuzzy funnel
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+torus"} noncommutative torus
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li
              Ludwik Dabrowski:
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Garden of Quantum Spheres
              %strong 61
              (2003) 37-48 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0212264"}> arXiv:math/0212264
              ,
